  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-random-field-stationarity-covariance-and-variogram": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $T\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, have its relative Borel sigma-algebra, and\nlet $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be one probability space. A **real random\nfield** indexed by $T$ is a family $X=(X(s))_{s\\in T}$ of real random variables\n$X(s):\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ on that space. This is coordinate measurability:\neach map $(s,\\omega)\\mapsto X(s,\\omega)$ with $s$ fixed is measurable in\n$\\omega$. The field is **jointly measurable** when the map\n$(s,\\omega)\\mapsto X(s,\\omega)$ from\n$(T\\times\\Omega,\\mathcal B(T)\\otimes\\mathcal F)$ to\n$(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R))$ is measurable. Joint measurability is an\nadditional hypothesis when a spatial Lebesgue integral is used; it is not part\nof coordinate measurability.\n\nWrite $\\mathcal L(X(s_1),\\ldots,X(s_n))$ for the pushforward law of a finite\ncoordinate tuple ([[def-stochastic-process-and-finite-dimensional-distributions]],\n[[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]]). The field is **strictly stationary** on $T$ when, for every\nfinite tuple $s_1,\\ldots,s_n\\in T$ and every $u\\in\\mathbb R^d$ for which\n$s_i+u\\in T$ for all $i$, these joint laws are invariant under the common\ntranslation $s_i\\mapsto s_i+u$. This extends the integer-shift convention for\ntime series in [[def-strict-and-weak-stationarity-for-time-series]] to spatial\nindices.\n\nWrite $T-T:=\\{t-s:s,t\\in T\\}$ for the set of displacements realized by pairs\nof sites.\n\nThe field is **wide stationary** when every $X(s)$ is square-integrable, its\nmean is one constant $m$, and there is a function $C:T-T\\to\\mathbb R$ such\nthat\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X(s),X(t))=C(t-s)\\qquad(s,t\\in T).$$\nFor a wide-stationary field on all of $\\mathbb R^d$, it is **wide isotropic**\nwhen $C(h)$ depends only on the Euclidean norm $\\|h\\|$.\n\nFor $h\\in T-T$ and any $s$ with $s,s+h\\in T$, put\n$\\Delta_hX(s):=X(s+h)-X(s)$. The field is **intrinsically stationary of order\ntwo** when for each $h\\in T-T$ the first and second raw moments of\n$\\Delta_hX(s)$ are finite and independent of the admissible starting site\n$s$. Here these moments are $\\mathbb E[\\Delta_hX(s)]$ and\n$\\mathbb E[(\\Delta_hX(s))^2]$. The common second moment defines the\n**variogram**\n$$\\gamma(h):=\\frac12\\mathbb E[(\\Delta_hX(s))^2].$$\nThe mean increment may depend on $h$ and need not be zero; no finite second\nmoment of the field levels themselves is required by this definition."
    ],
    "lem-random-field-covariance-kernel-is-positive-semidefinite": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $X=(X(s))_{s\\in T}$ be a real random field on one probability space, with\n$X(s)\\in L^2$ for every $s\\in T$, and put\n$K(s,t):=\\operatorname{Cov}(X(s),X(t))$. Then $K$ is symmetric and positive\nsemidefinite: for every $n\\ge1$, sites $s_1,\\ldots,s_n\\in T$, and real\n$a_1,\\ldots,a_n$,\n$$\\sum_{i=1}^n\\sum_{j=1}^n a_i a_j K(s_i,s_j)\\ge0.$$"
    ],
    "thm-gaussian-random-field-existence-from-a-positive-semidefinite-kernel": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. Let $T\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, be an\nindex set, let $m:T\\to\\mathbb R$, and let $K:T\\times T\\to\\mathbb R$ be a\nsymmetric positive-semidefinite kernel, meaning that every finite real\nquadratic form $\\sum_{i,j}a_i a_jK(t_i,t_j)$ is nonnegative. There is a unique\nprobability measure $\\mu$ on the cylinder sigma-algebra $\\mathcal C_T$ of\n$\\mathbb R^T$ whose nonempty finite-coordinate marginal on\n$F=\\{t_1,\\ldots,t_n\\}\\subseteq T$ is\n$$N_n\\big((m(t_i))_{i=1}^n,[K(t_i,t_j)]_{i,j=1}^n\\big).$$\nThe empty-coordinate marginal is the unique law on the singleton empty\nproduct. Under $\\mu$, the coordinate maps form a Gaussian field with mean\nfunction $m$ and covariance kernel $K$. No sample-path continuity or joint\nmeasurability is asserted."
    ],
    "thm-order-two-field-variogram-is-conditionally-negative-definite": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $X=(X(t))_{t\\in\\mathbb R^d}$ be order-two intrinsically stationary in the\nsense of [[def-random-field-stationarity-covariance-and-variogram]], with\nvariogram $\\gamma(h)=\\tfrac12\\mathbb E[(X(t+h)-X(t))^2]$. Then for every\n$n\\ge1$, sites $s_1,\\ldots,s_n\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and real coefficients\n$a_1,\\ldots,a_n$ with $\\sum_i a_i=0$,\n$$\\sum_{i=1}^n\\sum_{j=1}^n a_i a_j\\gamma(s_i-s_j)\\le0.$$\nThis is conditional negative definiteness over real coefficients. If $X$ is\nalso wide stationary with covariance function $C$, then\n$$\\gamma(h)=C(0)-C(h)\\qquad(h\\in\\mathbb R^d).$$\nThe proof is choice-free."
    ],
    "thm-continuous-stationary-field-covariance-has-bochner-measure": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice and Dependent Choice. Let\n$G=(\\mathbb R^d,+)$, $d\\ge1$, with its usual locally compact Hausdorff\ntopology, and let $X=(X(t))_{t\\in\\mathbb R^d}$ be a real wide-stationary\nfield whose covariance function\n$$C(h):=\\operatorname{Cov}(X(s),X(s+h))$$\nis continuous on $\\mathbb R^d$. There is a unique finite positive Radon measure\n$\\mu$ on the Pontryagin dual $\\widehat G$ such that\n$$C(h)=\\int_{\\widehat G}\\chi(h)\\,\\mu(d\\chi)\\qquad(h\\in\\mathbb R^d),$$\nand $\\mu(\\widehat G)=C(0)$. This is a measure representation and makes no\ndensity assertion."
    ],
    "ex-covariance-kernel-and-intrinsic-variogram-checks": []
  }
